Q: What Programming Pattern to use for a generic HTML table data extractor A: <p><strong>Short Answer</strong><br />
Yes. The usual and readable pattern here is <strong>single function + input-based dispatch</strong> (a.k.a. <em>strategy selection by shape</em>). Instead of a <code>filterOption</code> flag, infer the behavior from the type/structure of the second argument.</p>
<p><strong>Why</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p>JS is dynamically typed, so separate functions add friction</p>
</li>
<li><p>Flags (<code>filterOption</code>) are brittle and unclear</p>
</li>
<li><p>Checking the <em>shape</em> of the input is idiomatic and scales well</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Example pattern</strong></p>
<pre><code>function extractData(table, selector) {
  const data = [...table.rows].map(r =&gt;
    [...r.cells].map(c =&gt; c.innerText)
  );

  if (typeof selector === &quot;function&quot;) {
    return data.map(selector);
  }

  if (Array.isArray(selector) &amp;&amp; selector.every(b =&gt; typeof b === &quot;boolean&quot;)) {
    return data.map(row =&gt; row.filter((_, i) =&gt; selector[i]));
  }

  if (Array.isArray(selector) &amp;&amp; selector.every(n =&gt; Number.isInteger(n))) {
    return data.map(row =&gt; selector.map(i =&gt; row[i]));
  }

  if (typeof selector === &quot;string&quot;) {
    const headers = data[0];
    const indexes = selector.split(&quot;,&quot;).map(h =&gt; headers.indexOf(h.trim()));
    return data.map(row =&gt; indexes.map(i =&gt; row[i]));
  }

  return data;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Takeaway</strong><br />
This is a standard <em>dispatch-by-input-shape</em> approach: one function, no flags, behavior is obvious from the argument you pass.</p>

Q: Copy / Put text on the clipboard with FireFox, Safari and Chrome A: <p>Use the <code>navigator.clipboard</code> API. It is supported in the latest versions of Chrome, Firefox, Edge, and Safari.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>async function copyText(text) {
  document.body.focus()
  navigator.clipboard.writeText(text)
    .then(() =&gt; alert("Text copied to clipboard!"))
    .catch(err =&gt; alert("Failed to copy text: " + err))  
}

var txt = document.getElementById('txt');
var btn = document.getElementById('btn');
btn.addEventListener('click', function(){
  copyText(txt.value);
})</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;input id="txt" value="Hello World!" /&gt;
&lt;button id="btn"&gt;Copy To Clipboard&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Error testing a React/D3 component with Jest and react testing library A: <p>I'm in a slightly different setup with vitest instead of jest, and I was having slightly different issues, but I found the solution for this issue. I was able to reproduce this issue and and fix it like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-tsx prettyprint-override"><code>it(&quot;should test that the zoom completes&quot;, async () =&gt; {
    const TestComponent = () =&gt; {
      const [hasDragged, setHasZoomed] = useState(false);
      const ref = useRef&lt;SVGSVGElement&gt;(null);
      useLayoutEffect(() =&gt; {
        const zoomBehavior = zoom&lt;SVGSVGElement, unknown&gt;()
          .extent([
            [0, 0],
            [100, 100],
          ])
          .on(&quot;zoom&quot;, (event) =&gt; {
            console.log(event);
          })
          .on(&quot;end&quot;, (event) =&gt; {
            console.log(event);
            setHasZoomed(true);
          });
        if (ref.current) {
          select(ref.current)
            .call(zoomBehavior)
            .transition()
            .duration(500)
            .call(zoomBehavior.transform, new ZoomTransform(2, 0, 0));
        }
      });
      return (
        &lt;&gt;
          &lt;svg ref={ref} data-testid=&quot;el&quot;&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;
          {hasDragged &amp;&amp; &lt;Typography&gt;win&lt;/Typography&gt;}
        &lt;/&gt;
      );
    };
    render(&lt;TestComponent /&gt;);

    expect(await screen.findByText(/win/)).toBeInTheDocument();
  });
</code></pre>
<p>If I include the <code>.extent(...)</code> call in the zoomBehavior, the test passes, otherwise it gives the <code>could not find baseVal</code> error. In my non test code, I have a State named <code>dimensions</code> that holds the current size of the svg retrieved from <code>ref.current.getBoundingClientRef</code> and I provide the value <code>[[0,0],[dimensions.clientX, dimensions.clientY]]</code> to the <code>.extent</code> call. I don't call sleep in my code because <code>await screen.findByText</code> retries for 1000ms by default.</p>
<p>I found this post looking for a solution to a different problem, testing d3 drag events, so I thought I would add that here since I also fixed that issue. Basically, events were missing the view property which d3 required, so I fixed it like so (with a slightly different <code>TestComponent</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-tsx prettyprint-override"><code>    render(&lt;TestComponent /&gt;);
    const el = await screen.findByTestId(&quot;el&quot;);
    const window = el.ownerDocument.defaultView;
    const fix = (e: Event) =&gt; {
      Object.defineProperty(e, &quot;view&quot;, { value: window });
      return e;
    };
    el.dispatchEvent(
      fix(
        new MouseEvent(&quot;mousedown&quot;, {
          clientX: 10,
          clientY: 10,
        }),
      ),
    );
</code></pre>
<p>Since this doesn't use the userEvent parts of @testing-library, you have to manually fire all three events, the mousedown, mousemove, and mouseup. I couldn't figure out a way to get user.pointer to bind the view properly. If you try to pass the window into the view directly, it complains, but this worked.</p>
